import type { RichContent } from "../types";

/** Authored explanations for paradigm and classical-ML nodes, keyed by taxonomy node id. */
export const PARADIGM_EXPLANATIONS: Record<string, RichContent> = {
  ai: {
    explanation: `Artificial intelligence is the field of building computer systems that perform tasks we normally associate with human intelligence: recognizing objects, understanding language, making plans, solving problems, and learning from experience. It is not one technique but a broad research program that has produced several distinct families of methods over seventy years.

The field splits along one central question: should intelligence be programmed or learned? The symbolic tradition writes down knowledge explicitly as rules and logic that a machine can execute. The machine learning tradition instead shows a system many examples and lets it infer the patterns itself. Other traditions, such as probabilistic reasoning and evolutionary computation, offer their own answers. Modern systems often blend several of these.

Today the dominant approach is deep learning, a form of machine learning built on large neural networks, and it powers language models, image generators, and speech systems. But the older paradigms did not disappear. Rule engines still run business logic, probabilistic filters still guide spacecraft, and planners still schedule factory floors. Knowing the full map helps you pick the right tool rather than assuming everything is a neural network.`,
    analogy: "AI is like the field of transportation: cars, trains, bicycles, and planes all move people, but each was invented for different constraints, and the newest one does not make the others obsolete.",
    visualExample: "A single smartphone runs several AI paradigms at once: a neural network transcribes your voice, a probabilistic filter smooths your GPS position, and rule-based logic decides when to silence notifications.",
  },

  paradigms: {
    explanation: `A paradigm in AI is a school of thought about where machine intelligence should come from. Each paradigm makes a different bet. Symbolic AI bets on explicit rules and logic written by humans. Machine learning bets on statistical patterns extracted from data. Probabilistic AI bets on representing uncertainty with probability distributions. Evolutionary computation bets on random variation plus selection pressure. Fuzzy logic bets on reasoning with degrees of truth instead of hard true and false.

These bets lead to very different engineering trade-offs. Symbolic systems are transparent and auditable but brittle when the world does not match their rules. Learned systems are flexible and handle messy inputs but are opaque and need lots of data. Probabilistic systems quantify their own uncertainty but require you to model the problem carefully up front.

The history of AI is largely the history of these paradigms rising and falling in popularity. Symbolic AI dominated from the 1950s through the 1980s, statistical learning took over in the 1990s, and deep learning has led since the 2010s. In practice, serious systems mix paradigms: a self-driving car uses neural networks for perception, probabilistic filters for state estimation, and symbolic planners for route decisions.`,
    analogy: "Paradigms are like schools of medicine: surgery, pharmacology, and physical therapy all treat patients, but each starts from a different theory of what fixes the body, and a good hospital uses all of them.",
    visualExample: "A warehouse robot combines paradigms: deep learning identifies packages from camera images, a symbolic planner sequences its pick-up route, and a Kalman filter tracks its position between sensor readings.",
  },

  symbolic: {
    explanation: `Symbolic AI builds intelligence from explicit, human-readable representations: symbols standing for objects and concepts, plus formal rules for manipulating them. A symbolic program might represent the fact that all birds have wings as a logical statement, then derive new facts from it by applying inference rules. The knowledge is written by people, and the machine's job is to combine it correctly and quickly.

The mechanics center on knowledge representation and inference. Facts and rules live in a knowledge base. An inference engine applies rules to known facts to derive new conclusions, either working forward from data toward conclusions or backward from a goal toward supporting evidence. Because every step is an explicit rule application, you can trace exactly why the system reached any conclusion.

This transparency is symbolic AI's great strength and its data-free operation is another: no training set is required, just an expert willing to write down what they know. The weakness is brittleness. The real world is full of exceptions, ambiguity, and perceptual mess that resists clean rules, which is why symbolic systems struggled with vision and speech. This limitation, encountered hard in the 1980s, is what pushed the field toward learning from data. Today symbolic methods survive in business rule engines, theorem provers, query planners, and increasingly in hybrid neuro-symbolic systems that pair learned perception with logical reasoning.`,
    analogy: "Symbolic AI is like a law book plus a judge: all the rules are written down explicitly, and intelligence means applying the right rules to the facts of the case, with a fully traceable chain of reasoning.",
    visualExample: "Airline booking systems still run symbolic rule engines that encode fare rules, such as which ticket classes can be combined and when a change fee applies, because those rules must be exact and auditable.",
  },

  "expert-systems": {
    explanation: `An expert system captures the knowledge of a human specialist as a large collection of if-then rules, then applies those rules automatically to new cases. A medical expert system might contain a rule like: if the patient has a fever and a stiff neck and photophobia, then consider meningitis with high priority. Hundreds or thousands of such rules together approximate the specialist's judgment.

The architecture has two parts. The knowledge base holds the rules, written and refined through interviews with domain experts, a slow process called knowledge engineering. The inference engine matches rules against the facts of the current case and fires the ones whose conditions hold, potentially chaining many rules together to reach a conclusion. Most systems can also explain their reasoning by listing the chain of rules that fired, which matters enormously in domains like medicine and finance.

Expert systems were the first commercially successful AI, with systems like MYCIN for diagnosing blood infections and XCON for configuring computer orders in the 1980s. They declined because knowledge engineering was expensive, the rule bases became unmaintainable as they grew, and the systems failed abruptly on cases outside their rules. Their descendants live on as business rules engines and clinical decision support tools, and the core lesson, that expert knowledge can be operationalized, shaped everything after.`,
    analogy: "An expert system is like a very thorough checklist written by a retiring specialist so that junior staff can handle the routine ninety percent of cases exactly the way the expert would.",
    visualExample: "Insurance companies use rule-based systems to auto-approve routine claims: rules check policy coverage, claim amount, and fraud indicators, and only claims that fail a rule get routed to a human adjuster.",
  },

  "logic-prog": {
    explanation: `Logic programming is a style of computation where you state facts and logical rules, then ask questions, and the language itself figures out the answer by formal inference. Instead of writing step-by-step instructions, you declare what is true. In Prolog, the best-known logic language, you might state that Tom is the parent of Bob, that Bob is the parent of Ann, and that a grandparent is a parent of a parent. Asking who is Ann's grandparent then requires no further code; the system derives Tom.

The engine behind this is called resolution with unification. Given a query, the system searches for chains of rules and facts that would make the query true, systematically trying to match variables against known values and backtracking when a path fails. Computation becomes proof search: the answer to your question is a successful proof, and the variable bindings found along the way are the output.

This declarative style shines when the problem is naturally relational: querying databases, checking constraints, analyzing program code, or reasoning about family trees, schedules, and permissions. Datalog, a restricted cousin of Prolog, powers modern static analysis tools and some database engines. The approach struggles when problems need heavy numeric computation or when the search space explodes, which is why logic programming settled into specialized niches rather than becoming general purpose.`,
    analogy: "Logic programming is like asking a meticulous librarian a question: you never tell them how to search, you just ask, and they exhaustively follow cross-references through the catalog until they can either prove an answer or prove there is none.",
    visualExample: "The static analysis tool behind many code security scanners is written in Datalog: facts describe every function call and data flow in your codebase, and rules like 'user input reaching a SQL query is an injection risk' find vulnerabilities by pure inference.",
  },

  planning: {
    explanation: `Automated planning and reasoning is the study of getting a machine to derive new conclusions or to construct a sequence of actions that achieves a goal. A planner is given three things: a description of the current state of the world, a set of available actions with their preconditions and effects, and a goal condition. It searches for an ordered sequence of actions that transforms the current state into one satisfying the goal.

The classic formulation is STRIPS, where states are sets of logical facts and each action deletes some facts and adds others. Planning then becomes a search problem through the space of reachable states, guided by heuristics that estimate how far a state is from the goal. A related family, SAT and constraint solvers, encodes a reasoning problem as a huge Boolean formula and applies extremely optimized algorithms to find a satisfying assignment, effectively answering whether a configuration or plan can exist at all.

These techniques matter wherever correctness and lookahead are non-negotiable. Spacecraft schedule their own operations with planners, logistics companies sequence deliveries, chip designers verify circuits with SAT solvers, and package managers resolve dependency versions the same way. Modern research connects planning back to language models, using an LLM to propose candidate steps while a formal planner or verifier guarantees the result is actually valid.`,
    analogy: "A planner works like someone solving a puzzle by thinking several moves ahead: it does not act, it simulates chains of actions in its head and commits only to a sequence it can prove reaches the goal.",
    visualExample: "NASA's Deep Space 1 probe ran an onboard planner that autonomously sequenced engine burns, camera operations, and communication windows, replanning on its own when a thruster fault invalidated the original schedule.",
  },

  bayesian: {
    explanation: `Probabilistic AI represents knowledge as probability distributions and treats reasoning as the calculation of how those probabilities change when new evidence arrives. Instead of asserting that a patient has the flu, a Bayesian system says there is a 70 percent probability of flu given the observed symptoms, and it revises that number as test results come in. The updating rule is Bayes' theorem, which prescribes exactly how a prior belief should shift in light of evidence.

The workhorse structures are graphical models. A Bayesian network is a directed graph where nodes are variables and edges encode direct probabilistic influence, letting you represent a complex joint distribution compactly and query any part of it. Hidden Markov models handle sequences where the true state is hidden and only noisy observations are visible. Kalman filters do the same for continuous quantities like position and velocity. Gaussian processes put distributions over entire functions, giving predictions with built-in error bars.

The defining advantage is calibrated uncertainty. A probabilistic model does not just answer, it tells you how confident to be, which is essential in medicine, robotics, finance, and any setting where acting on a wrong guess is costly. The cost is modeling effort and computation: you must specify the structure and priors, and exact inference is intractable for large models, forcing approximations. Probabilistic thinking also underlies much of machine learning itself, since most loss functions are disguised likelihoods.`,
    analogy: "Bayesian reasoning is how a good detective works: start with prior suspicions, weigh each new clue by how much more likely it is under one theory than another, and keep a running degree of belief rather than jumping to a verdict.",
    visualExample: "Your phone's GPS uses a Kalman filter: satellite fixes are noisy and intermittent, so the filter maintains a probability distribution over your true position and velocity, blending each new fix with its prediction to draw the smooth blue dot.",
  },

  evolutionary: {
    explanation: `Evolutionary computation solves optimization problems by mimicking natural selection. You maintain a population of candidate solutions, evaluate each one with a fitness function, keep the best performers, and produce the next generation by mutating them and recombining pieces of different parents. Over many generations, the population drifts toward better solutions without anyone specifying how a good solution should be constructed.

The mechanics are simple and general. A genetic algorithm encodes each candidate as a string of parameters, applies crossover to mix two parents and mutation to perturb offspring randomly, then selects survivors in proportion to fitness. Variants extend the idea: NEAT evolves neural network topologies, growing structure over generations, and CMA-ES adapts the shape of its mutation distribution to the local landscape, making it one of the strongest tools for continuous optimization without gradients.

The key requirement is only that you can score a candidate. You need no gradient, no smoothness, not even a mathematical description of the objective. This makes evolutionary methods valuable for problems where gradient descent cannot operate: evolving antenna shapes, tuning simulator parameters, designing molecules, or searching neural architecture spaces. The trade-off is sample efficiency; evaluating thousands of candidates per generation is expensive, so where gradients exist, gradient methods almost always win.`,
    analogy: "Evolutionary computation is like breeding racehorses without knowing any genetics: you cannot design a fast horse from first principles, but you can race them all, breed the winners, and let speed emerge over generations.",
    visualExample: "NASA evolved the antenna for its ST5 spacecraft with a genetic algorithm: the resulting bent-wire design looked like nothing a human would draw, but it outperformed hand-engineered antennas on the mission's requirements.",
  },

  fuzzy: {
    explanation: `Fuzzy logic extends classical logic so statements can be partially true. Instead of forcing a room to be either hot or not hot, fuzzy logic lets it be hot to degree 0.7. This matters because many useful human concepts, like warm, tall, fast, and near, have no sharp boundary, yet people reason with them effectively. Fuzzy logic gives machines the same ability.

The machinery works in three stages. First, fuzzification maps a crisp measurement, say 27 degrees, onto membership degrees in linguistic categories, perhaps 0.7 hot and 0.3 warm, using curves called membership functions. Second, a set of fuzzy rules fires, each to the degree its conditions hold: if the room is hot and humidity is high, then set the fan to fast. Third, defuzzification blends the weighted conclusions of all fired rules into a single crisp output, such as a fan speed of 78 percent.

The payoff is smooth control from readable rules. A dozen fuzzy rules written in plain language can replace a differential equation model of a system, and the output changes gradually rather than snapping between discrete modes. Fuzzy controllers became standard in consumer appliances, especially in Japan in the 1990s, and still run in rice cookers, washing machines, camera autofocus, and vehicle transmissions. It is a control and reasoning tool rather than a learning method, and it is distinct from probability: fuzziness measures degree of membership in a vague concept, not the chance of an event.`,
    analogy: "Fuzzy logic is like a dimmer switch replacing a light switch: the question stops being on or off and becomes how much, which matches how people actually describe the world.",
    visualExample: "A fuzzy rice cooker measures temperature and moisture continuously and applies rules like 'if rice is slightly underdone and moisture is low, extend cooking a little,' adjusting heat smoothly instead of just switching between cook and warm.",
  },

  ml: {
    explanation: `Machine learning builds systems whose behavior is learned from data rather than programmed by hand. Instead of writing rules for recognizing spam, you collect thousands of emails labeled spam or not spam, and an algorithm finds the statistical patterns that separate them. The output of this process is a model: a function with tunable parameters whose values were chosen to fit the data.

Training follows a common recipe across almost all of ML. Define a model family, such as a linear function, a tree, or a neural network. Define a loss function that measures how wrong the model's predictions are on the training data. Then adjust the parameters to reduce the loss, often using gradient descent, which repeatedly nudges each parameter in the direction that decreases error. The subtle part is generalization: the goal is not to memorize the training set but to perform well on new data, which is why practitioners hold out test data the model never sees during training and guard against overfitting.

Machine learning took over from symbolic AI because so many real problems resist explicit rules. Nobody can write down the rules that distinguish a cat photo from a dog photo, but a model can learn them from a million examples. The paradigm's requirements are its main constraints: you need enough representative data, a meaningful signal to learn from, and tolerance for a system that is statistical rather than exact and whose reasoning is harder to inspect than a rule base.`,
    analogy: "Machine learning is teaching by example instead of by instruction: rather than handing someone a rulebook for judging wine, you have them taste a thousand labeled bottles until their judgment converges on the experts'.",
    visualExample: "Email spam filters are classic ML: trained on millions of user-labeled messages, the model learns which combinations of sender patterns, phrases, and link structures indicate spam, and it keeps adapting as spammers change tactics.",
  },

  "learning-signals": {
    explanation: `A learning signal is the feedback that tells a model whether it is doing well during training, and its form defines the major branches of machine learning. The same network architecture can be trained with any of them; what differs is where the notion of correct comes from.

Supervised learning provides the strongest signal: every training input comes with the correct output, so the model always knows exactly how wrong it was. Unsupervised learning provides no answers at all; the model must find structure, like clusters or compact representations, on its own. Self-supervised learning manufactures labels from the raw data itself, for example by hiding the next word and asking the model to predict it. Semi-supervised learning stretches a small labeled set using a large unlabeled one. Reinforcement learning provides only a reward, a score that arrives after actions are taken, without ever saying which action was correct.

The practical importance is economic. Labels are expensive because humans must produce them, while raw data is nearly free. The history of recent AI is largely the story of moving down this cost curve: modern language models are pretrained with self-supervision on trillions of freely available tokens, then refined with small amounts of expensive supervised and reinforcement feedback. Choosing a learning signal is usually the first design decision in any ML project, dictated by what data you can actually obtain.`,
    analogy: "Learning signals are different teachers: one hands you an answer key (supervised), one gives you a pile of books and no syllabus (unsupervised), one makes you quiz yourself by covering up words (self-supervised), and one only tells you your final grade (reinforcement).",
    visualExample: "Building a photo organizer shows the choice concretely: label ten thousand photos by hand and train supervised, or run unsupervised clustering to group similar faces with no labels, or fine-tune a self-supervised vision model that learned from unlabeled internet images.",
  },

  supervised: {
    explanation: `Supervised learning trains a model on pairs of inputs and correct outputs. Show it thousands of houses with their sale prices, and it learns to predict the price of a new house. Show it millions of images tagged cat or dog, and it learns to classify new images. The labels act as a teacher: for every training example, the model can compare its prediction against the truth and measure exactly how wrong it was.

Training is error-driven adjustment. The model makes predictions on training examples, a loss function quantifies the gap between predictions and labels, and an optimization procedure updates the model's parameters to shrink that gap. Repeated over the whole dataset many times, this drives the model toward a function that maps inputs to correct outputs. The two canonical task types are classification, where the output is a category, and regression, where it is a number. The central danger is overfitting, where the model memorizes the training set instead of learning the underlying pattern, which is caught by evaluating on held-out data.

Supervised learning is the most reliable and most widely deployed form of ML. When you can get good labels, it typically beats every alternative, and most production ML, from fraud detection to medical imaging to ad ranking, is supervised. Its binding constraint is the labels themselves: they are costly, sometimes inconsistent, and the model can only ever be as good as the labeling it learned from.`,
    analogy: "Supervised learning is studying with flashcards: question on the front, answer on the back, and after thousands of self-corrections you can answer questions you have never seen.",
    visualExample: "Radiology AI is trained on hundreds of thousands of X-rays that radiologists have annotated with findings; the model learns the mapping from pixels to diagnoses and can then flag likely pneumonia in scans it has never encountered.",
  },

  unsupervised: {
    explanation: `Unsupervised learning finds structure in data that has no labels. There is no correct answer to predict; instead the algorithm looks for regularities: which examples are similar to each other, which features vary together, which few dimensions capture most of the variation, which points look anomalous. The data itself is the only teacher.

The main task families are clustering, which partitions data into groups of similar items, as k-means does; dimensionality reduction, which compresses many features into a few informative ones, as PCA does; density estimation, which models where data is common versus rare, enabling anomaly detection; and association mining, which finds items that co-occur. What counts as good structure depends on a similarity measure or an objective the practitioner chooses, which makes evaluation inherently harder than in supervised learning. Two different clusterings of the same customers can both be defensible.

Unsupervised methods matter because unlabeled data vastly outnumbers labeled data in every organization. They are the standard first move in exploratory analysis, revealing segments and outliers before anyone decides what to predict. They also do quiet production work: grouping similar news articles, detecting fraudulent transactions as density outliers, and compressing features before a supervised model. Conceptually, unsupervised learning was also the precursor to self-supervised learning, which found a way to turn unlabeled data into an explicit training signal.`,
    analogy: "Unsupervised learning is sorting a stranger's massive photo collection with no captions: you naturally group by faces, places, and events, discovering the categories yourself rather than being told what they are.",
    visualExample: "Retailers cluster their customers by purchase history without any predefined segments: the algorithm might surface groups like weekly bulk-buyers, seasonal gift shoppers, and discount-only visitors, which then shape marketing strategy.",
  },

  selfsup: {
    explanation: `Self-supervised learning creates its own labels from raw, unlabeled data. The trick is to hide part of the data and train the model to predict the hidden part from what remains. Delete the next word of a sentence and predict it. Mask patches of an image and reconstruct them. Because the hidden part is right there in the data, every document, image, and audio clip becomes a training example with a free, perfect label. No human annotation is needed.

Mechanically, this is supervised learning where the supervision is manufactured. A language model trained to predict the next token computes a probability over its vocabulary, is scored against the actual next token, and adjusts its weights, exactly like any supervised classifier. The magic is in what the model must learn to do this well: predicting the next word in arbitrary text requires absorbing grammar, facts, style, and a surprising amount of reasoning. The pretext task is trivial to state, but solving it at scale forces the model to build rich internal representations of the domain.

This is the engine of the modern AI era. GPT-style models are self-supervised next-token predictors trained on trillions of tokens; BERT-style models predict masked words; contrastive vision models like CLIP align images with their captions. Self-supervision broke the label bottleneck that had capped ML for decades, letting models learn from essentially all available text and imagery, after which a small amount of supervised fine-tuning adapts them to specific tasks.`,
    analogy: "Self-supervised learning is reading with your thumb over the next word and guessing it before moving on: nobody grades you, the book itself is the answer key, and doing this across a whole library quietly teaches you the language.",
    visualExample: "Every large language model you have used was pretrained this way: shown trillions of sentences from the web with the next token hidden, it learned grammar, world knowledge, and reasoning purely from the prediction game, before any human feedback was applied.",
  },

  semisup: {
    explanation: `Semi-supervised learning trains a model with a small labeled dataset plus a much larger unlabeled one. This matches how data actually arrives: labeling requires paid human effort, so you might afford a thousand labeled examples while sitting on a million unlabeled ones. Semi-supervised methods let the unlabeled majority sharpen what the labeled minority teaches.

The unlabeled data helps because it reveals the shape of the data distribution. Two common mechanisms are pseudo-labeling and consistency regularization. In pseudo-labeling, a model trained on the labeled set predicts labels for unlabeled examples, its most confident predictions are treated as new training labels, and training repeats, letting the model teach itself outward from the labeled core. Consistency regularization instead perturbs an unlabeled example, by adding noise or augmenting an image, and penalizes the model if its prediction changes, pushing decision boundaries into low-density regions between natural clusters rather than through the middle of them.

The approach shines when labels are scarce and expensive, as in medical imaging, where expert annotation costs specialist time, or in speech and rare-language processing. Its risk is confirmation bias: if early pseudo-labels are wrong, the model can confidently reinforce its own mistakes, so confidence thresholds and careful validation matter. In the modern stack, the same core idea persists as pretraining on unlabeled data followed by fine-tuning on a small labeled set.`,
    analogy: "Semi-supervised learning is learning to sort mushrooms from a guide who identifies only twenty specimens, then studying a full basket on your own: the unlabeled mushrooms show you which features vary together, making those twenty labels go much further.",
    visualExample: "A hospital building a tumor detector can only afford radiologist annotations for two thousand scans, but has two hundred thousand unlabeled ones; semi-supervised training uses the unlabeled scans to learn what normal anatomy looks like, substantially improving accuracy over the labeled set alone.",
  },

  rl: {
    explanation: `Reinforcement learning trains an agent through trial and error. The agent observes the state of an environment, chooses an action, and receives a reward signal plus a new state. Nobody tells it which action was correct; it only sees numerical rewards, often delayed and sparse. Its goal is to learn a policy, a mapping from states to actions, that maximizes total reward over time.

Two difficulties define the field. The credit assignment problem: when a chess agent wins after sixty moves, which moves deserve the credit? Rewards must be propagated backward through long sequences of decisions. The exploration-exploitation trade-off: the agent must balance exploiting actions it already knows are good against exploring new ones that might be better, since it can only learn about actions by trying them. Algorithms split into value-based methods like Q-learning, which learn how much future reward each action is worth, and policy-based methods like PPO, which adjust the action-choosing policy directly.

Reinforcement learning fits problems that are sequential and interactive, where each decision changes the situation facing the next one. It produced landmark results like AlphaGo and superhuman Atari play, and it controls real systems from data center cooling to robot locomotion. Its most visible modern role is in aligning language models: RLHF treats the LLM as a policy, human preference scores as reward, and fine-tunes the model to produce responses people rate highly. RL remains sample-hungry and finicky to train, so it is applied where simulation is cheap or the payoff justifies the effort.`,
    analogy: "Reinforcement learning is how you learned to ride a bicycle: no one could tell you the correct torque for each muscle, you just tried, fell, adjusted, and gradually your body learned a policy that keeps you upright.",
    visualExample: "AlphaGo learned Go by playing millions of games against itself, receiving reward only for winning; with no human telling it which moves were good, it discovered strategies that surprised professional players, including the famous move 37 against Lee Sedol.",
  },

  qlearning: {
    explanation: `Q-learning learns the value of taking each action in each state. The Q in the name stands for quality: Q(state, action) estimates the total future reward an agent will collect if it takes that action from that state and behaves well afterward. Once these values are accurate, acting optimally is trivial: in any state, pick the action with the highest Q-value.

Learning proceeds by bootstrapping. After taking an action and observing the reward and the next state, the agent updates its estimate using the Bellman relationship: the value of this action should equal the immediate reward plus the discounted value of the best action available in the next state. Each experience nudges the stored estimate toward that target. Remarkably, this converges to correct values even while the agent explores with imperfect behavior, because the update always references the best next action rather than the one actually taken. In small problems the Q-values live in a lookup table; in large ones a neural network approximates the function, which is the idea behind Deep Q-Networks, the algorithm that learned Atari games from raw pixels in 2013 and helped launch deep reinforcement learning.

Q-learning suits problems with discrete actions, such as moving in a grid, choosing a menu of options, or pressing game buttons. Its limits appear with continuous actions, where taking a maximum over actions becomes impractical, which is where policy gradient methods take over.`,
    analogy: "Q-learning is like building a mental price tag for every choice at every crossroads: after enough trips, you know exactly what each turn is worth, so navigating well becomes just reading the tags.",
    visualExample: "The 2013 DeepMind Atari result used Q-learning with a neural network: given only screen pixels and the score, it learned Q-values for each joystick action, eventually discovering strategies like tunneling through the wall in Breakout.",
  },

  "policy-grad": {
    explanation: `Policy gradient methods learn the agent's behavior directly. Instead of learning values for actions and deriving behavior from them, the policy itself is a parameterized function, usually a neural network, that maps states to a probability distribution over actions. Training adjusts the network's weights to make high-reward actions more probable and low-reward actions less probable.

The core mechanism is elegantly simple: run the policy, collect trajectories of states, actions, and rewards, then compute a gradient that increases the log-probability of each action in proportion to how much reward followed it. Actions followed by better-than-expected outcomes get reinforced; worse-than-expected ones get suppressed. Raw policy gradients are noisy and unstable, so practical algorithms add stabilizers. PPO, the dominant method, clips each update so the new policy cannot move too far from the old one in a single step, preventing the catastrophic collapses that plagued earlier approaches. Actor-critic variants like A2C pair the policy (actor) with a learned value estimator (critic) that reduces variance in the updates.

Policy gradients handle what Q-learning cannot: continuous action spaces like joint torques on a robot, and naturally stochastic policies. PPO's reliability made it the workhorse of applied RL, and it is the algorithm behind RLHF: the language model is the policy, its generated text is the action, and a reward model trained on human preferences supplies the reward that shapes it.`,
    analogy: "Policy gradient learning is like refining a golf swing: you do not compute the value of every possible arm angle, you take swings, feel the results, and directly adjust the motion, reinforcing what worked.",
    visualExample: "When a chat model is aligned with RLHF, PPO does the tuning: the model generates responses, a reward model scores them for helpfulness, and PPO shifts the model's weights so highly scored response styles become more likely, within a clipped step size that keeps training stable.",
  },

  bandit: {
    explanation: `The multi-armed bandit is reinforcement learning stripped to a single repeated decision. Picture a row of slot machines, each with an unknown payout rate. Every round you pick one machine, observe the reward, and repeat. There are no states and no long-term consequences; the entire problem is deciding which arm to pull next, given everything observed so far.

This isolates the exploration-exploitation dilemma in its purest form. Always pulling the best-looking arm risks locking onto a mediocre one that got lucky early; always exploring wastes pulls on arms already known to be bad. Classic strategies formalize the balance. Epsilon-greedy exploits the best arm most of the time but explores randomly with small probability. Upper Confidence Bound picks the arm with the highest optimistic estimate, so uncertain arms get tried until their uncertainty shrinks. Thompson sampling keeps a probability distribution over each arm's payout and samples from those beliefs, exploring in exact proportion to remaining uncertainty. Performance is measured as regret, the reward lost relative to always pulling the best arm.

Bandits are among the most deployed algorithms in industry because so many problems fit the template: which headline to show, which ad to serve, which recommendation to surface, which treatment arm to assign in an adaptive clinical trial. Contextual bandits extend the model by letting the choice depend on features of the current user or situation, sitting one step below full reinforcement learning.`,
    analogy: "The bandit problem is choosing a lunch spot in a new city: keep going to the good place you found on day two, or gamble on the untried restaurant that might be better? Every lunch spent exploring is a lunch not spent at your known favorite.",
    visualExample: "News sites run headline tests as bandits: rather than a fixed A/B split, the algorithm shifts traffic toward the headline earning more clicks in real time, so a losing variant stops wasting impressions within hours.",
  },

  "classical-ml": {
    explanation: `Classical machine learning covers the algorithm families developed largely before the deep learning era: linear models, decision trees and their ensembles, support vector machines, nearest-neighbor methods, clustering, and dimensionality reduction. They share a common profile: relatively few parameters, fast training on ordinary hardware, and a reliance on well-prepared input features rather than learning representations from raw data.

That last point is the deepest contrast with deep learning. A classical model cannot look at raw pixels or raw text and discover useful features; a human must first engineer inputs like ratios, counts, and encodings. But when data already arrives as a table of meaningful columns, which describes most business data, that limitation disappears, and the classical toolkit is extremely effective. Gradient-boosted trees in particular still win most competitions and production benchmarks on tabular data, frequently beating neural networks while training in minutes.

The classical toolkit earns its place through economics and trust. These models train in seconds to minutes, run cheaply, work with hundreds of examples instead of millions, and are far easier to interpret and debug: you can read a decision tree, inspect regression coefficients, and explain a prediction to a regulator. The professional default is to try a simple classical baseline first; it either solves the problem outright or establishes the bar a more complex model must clearly beat.`,
    analogy: "Classical ML is the hand-tool workshop next to deep learning's factory: the factory wins at industrial scale, but for most everyday jobs the hand tools are faster to set up, cheaper to run, and you can see exactly what they are doing.",
    visualExample: "A bank's credit risk model is typically gradient boosting or logistic regression over engineered features like income ratio and payment history, chosen over a neural network because it performs as well on tabular data and its decisions can be explained to auditors.",
  },

  linreg: {
    explanation: `Linear regression predicts a number by taking a weighted sum of the input features plus a constant. Predicted house price might be 150 times the square footage, plus 8000 per bedroom, minus 500 per mile from the city center, plus a base offset. Training finds the weights that minimize squared prediction error across the training data, a problem so well-behaved it has an exact closed-form solution.

Logistic regression adapts this to classification. It computes the same weighted sum but passes it through the sigmoid function, which squashes any number into the range between 0 and 1, yielding a probability. A score of 0 maps to probability 0.5, large positive scores approach 1, large negatives approach 0. Training maximizes the likelihood of the observed labels. Despite the name, logistic regression is a classifier, and it remains one of the most deployed models in existence.

The strength of both is interpretability and honesty. Each weight states plainly how much one feature moves the prediction, and confidence intervals on weights are standard statistical machinery. The limitation is the linearity assumption: real relationships that curve or interact are invisible unless you manually add transformed features. Reach for linear or logistic regression when you need a fast, interpretable baseline, when explaining each feature's effect matters as much as accuracy, or when data is too scarce to support anything more flexible.`,
    analogy: "Linear regression is a recipe with adjustable ingredient amounts: prediction equals so much of this plus so much of that, and training is taste-testing against known dishes until the proportions are right.",
    visualExample: "Credit scoring is largely logistic regression: features like payment history, utilization, and account age each get a learned weight, and the resulting default probability is auditable because every factor's contribution can be read directly from the model.",
  },

  trees: {
    explanation: `A decision tree classifies or predicts by asking a sequence of yes-or-no questions about the features. Is income above 50,000? If yes, is the debt ratio below 0.3? Each question splits the data, and each path from the root ends at a leaf holding a prediction. The model is literally a flowchart, which makes it one of the only ML models a non-technical person can read directly.

Training grows the tree greedily from the root. At each node, the algorithm evaluates every possible feature-and-threshold split and picks the one that best purifies the resulting groups, measured by criteria like Gini impurity or entropy for classification, or variance reduction for regression. It recurses on each branch until leaves are pure or a size limit is hit. Left unchecked, a tree will grow until it memorizes the training data perfectly, so depth limits, minimum leaf sizes, and pruning are essential to make it generalize.

Trees handle mixed numeric and categorical features, ignore feature scaling, capture non-linear relationships and interactions automatically, and tolerate missing values. Their weakness is instability and mediocre accuracy alone: a small change in training data can produce a completely different tree. This is precisely why ensembles exist; random forests and gradient boosting are built out of many trees and fix the variance problem. Reach for a single decision tree when interpretability is the top priority and you need a model whose every decision can be traced and defended, accepting some accuracy loss versus an ensemble.`,
    analogy: "A decision tree is the game twenty questions turned into a model: each question is chosen to slice away as much uncertainty as possible, and the answers walk you down to a conclusion.",
    visualExample: "Emergency room triage protocols are decision trees in practice: is the patient breathing, is chest pain present, is blood pressure below a threshold, with each answer routing to the next question and finally to a priority level, fully traceable after the fact.",
  },

  rf: {
    explanation: `A random forest trains many decision trees and lets them vote. Each individual tree overfits its training data in its own way, but if the trees make different mistakes, averaging hundreds of them cancels the noise while keeping the signal. The result is dramatically more accurate and more stable than any single tree.

The design hinges on making the trees genuinely different, or decorrelated. Two sources of randomness accomplish this. First, bagging: each tree trains on a bootstrap sample, drawn from the training set with replacement, so every tree sees a slightly different dataset. Second, feature randomness: at every split, the tree may only consider a random subset of features, which prevents all trees from leaning on the same few dominant variables. At prediction time, classification takes the majority vote and regression averages the trees' outputs. A free bonus is out-of-bag evaluation: since each tree skipped about a third of the data, those held-out points give an honest accuracy estimate without a separate validation set.

Random forests are famously hard to misuse. They have few hyperparameters that matter, resist overfitting as more trees are added, handle mixed feature types without scaling, and provide useful feature importance rankings. The costs are model size, slower predictions than a single tree, and loss of the single tree's readability. Reach for a random forest when you want strong tabular accuracy with minimal tuning effort, especially as a robust first serious model before deciding whether gradient boosting's extra tuning is worth it.`,
    analogy: "A random forest is a jury instead of a single judge: each juror saw slightly different evidence and has personal biases, but their collective verdict is far more reliable than any individual's.",
    visualExample: "The Kinect sensor for Xbox used a random forest to identify body parts: for each depth-image pixel, hundreds of trees voted on whether it belonged to a hand, head, or torso, running in real time to track players' skeletons.",
  },

  gbm: {
    explanation: `Gradient boosting builds an ensemble of trees sequentially, where each new tree is trained to correct the errors of all the trees before it. The first tree makes rough predictions. The second tree is fit not to the labels but to the residual errors of the first. The third fits what the first two still get wrong, and so on for hundreds of rounds. The final prediction is the sum of all trees' contributions, each scaled by a small learning rate that keeps any single tree from dominating.

The name comes from viewing this as gradient descent in function space: each new tree points in the direction that most reduces the loss function, so the ensemble as a whole descends the loss the way gradient descent descends it for a neural network. The trees are deliberately shallow, typically a few levels deep, because each only needs to capture a small correction. Because boosting keeps fitting residuals, it can overfit if run too long, so the learning rate, tree depth, and number of rounds must be tuned together, usually with early stopping on a validation set.

Modern implementations, XGBoost, LightGBM, and CatBoost, add regularization, clever handling of categorical features and missing values, and extreme speed. On tabular data they are the reigning champions, winning most Kaggle competitions and powering countless production systems in finance, insurance, search ranking, and fraud detection, routinely edging out neural networks. Reach for gradient boosting when you have tabular data and accuracy is the priority; it is usually the strongest model available, provided you budget time for hyperparameter tuning.`,
    analogy: "Gradient boosting is drafting an essay through many rounds of edits: the first draft is rough, and each editing pass fixes specifically what the previous version got wrong, so hundreds of small corrections compound into polished work.",
    visualExample: "When a payment network scores a transaction for fraud in milliseconds, a gradient boosted model is typically doing it: hundreds of shallow trees over features like amount, merchant type, and recent account behavior sum to a fraud probability that decides whether your card is declined.",
  },

  svm: {
    explanation: `A support vector machine separates two classes by finding the boundary with the widest possible margin, the maximum distance to the nearest training points on each side. Many boundaries might separate the training data, but the max-margin one is provably the safest bet for new data, since points near a thin margin could easily fall on the wrong side. The few training points that touch the margin are the support vectors; they alone define the boundary, and every other point could be deleted without changing the model.

The kernel trick gives SVMs their real power. Data that is not linearly separable in its original space often becomes separable in a higher-dimensional one. Kernels let the SVM compute similarities as if the data had been mapped into that richer space, without ever paying the cost of the mapping. The radial basis function kernel, the most common choice, effectively measures closeness in an infinite-dimensional space, letting the SVM carve smooth, curved boundaries. A soft-margin parameter tolerates some misclassified points, trading training accuracy for a wider, more generalizable margin.

SVMs dominated machine learning through the 2000s, powering text classification, handwriting recognition, and bioinformatics, before deep learning and boosted trees displaced them from most niches. They still excel on small-to-medium datasets, especially with many features and few examples, as in genomics. Their main costs are training time that scales poorly past tens of thousands of examples and outputs that are not natural probabilities. Reach for an SVM when your dataset is modest in size but high-dimensional, and a well-tuned kernel can outperform tree ensembles.`,
    analogy: "An SVM builds the widest possible road between two neighborhoods: the route is fixed entirely by the few houses closest to the roadside (the support vectors), and a wide buffer means new construction is unlikely to end up on the wrong side.",
    visualExample: "Early spam filters and text classifiers were SVMs over word-count vectors: with tens of thousands of word features but modest training sets, the max-margin boundary generalized better than most alternatives of the era, and the approach still holds up for small document-classification tasks.",
  },

  knn: {
    explanation: `k-nearest neighbors classifies a new example by finding the k most similar examples in the training data and taking a vote among their labels. To classify a new fruit, find the five most similar known fruits by weight, color, and size; if four are apples, predict apple. For regression, average the neighbors' values instead of voting. There is no training phase at all: the model is simply the stored dataset, and all computation happens at prediction time.

Everything hinges on two choices. The distance metric defines what similar means, usually Euclidean distance, and because raw distances are dominated by features with large numeric ranges, feature scaling is mandatory. The value of k controls smoothness: k of 1 makes predictions hypersensitive to noise, since one mislabeled neighbor flips the answer, while very large k blurs real distinctions by averaging over unrelated points. The method's honesty is also its burden: it faithfully reflects local structure in the data, but it must search the training set for every prediction, which gets slow at scale, and it degrades in high dimensions where distances between points become nearly uniform, a symptom of the curse of dimensionality.

Reach for kNN when you need a quick baseline with essentially zero training effort, when the dataset is small enough for fast lookup, or when local similarity genuinely defines the answer. Its core idea, finding nearest vectors, has an outsized modern legacy: semantic search and retrieval-augmented generation run on approximate nearest-neighbor search over embeddings, which is kNN industrialized.`,
    analogy: "kNN is asking your five nearest neighbors how they vote and going with the majority: no theory, no learning, just the assumption that people who live near each other tend to vote alike.",
    visualExample: "A movie recommender can run on kNN: find the twenty users whose rating history is most similar to yours, and recommend what they loved that you have not seen. The same nearest-neighbor machinery, applied to embeddings, powers modern vector search.",
  },

  kmeans: {
    explanation: `k-means partitions unlabeled data into k clusters, grouping points so that each is close to the center of its own cluster. You choose k in advance, and the algorithm finds where the natural groupings lie. It is usually the first clustering method anyone tries, because it is simple, fast, and scales to millions of points.

The algorithm alternates between two steps until nothing changes. First, assign every point to its nearest cluster center. Second, move each center to the average position of the points assigned to it. Each iteration reduces the total within-cluster distance, so convergence is guaranteed, though only to a local optimum: a bad starting position can yield a poor clustering, which is why the k-means++ initialization, spreading initial centers apart, and running multiple restarts are standard practice. Choosing k itself requires judgment, commonly guided by the elbow method, which plots clustering quality against k and looks for the point of diminishing returns.

The assumptions are worth knowing: k-means favors compact, roughly spherical clusters of similar size, because assignment is by straight-line distance to a center. Crescent-shaped, nested, or wildly unequal clusters break it, and features must be scaled so no dimension dominates the distance. Reach for k-means when you need a fast first look at the group structure of unlabeled data, when clusters are plausibly blob-like, or for workhorse jobs like customer segmentation, image color quantization, and grouping embeddings.`,
    analogy: "k-means works like planning k food trucks in a city: place them, let every resident go to the nearest truck, then move each truck to the center of its crowd, and repeat until the trucks stop moving.",
    visualExample: "Reducing an image to a 16-color palette is k-means with k equal to 16: every pixel's RGB value is a point, the algorithm finds the 16 most representative colors, and each pixel is repainted with its cluster's center color.",
  },

  pca: {
    explanation: `Principal component analysis compresses a dataset with many features into a few new features that retain as much of the original variation as possible. The new features, called principal components, are weighted combinations of the originals, chosen so the first component points along the direction of greatest variance in the data, the second along the greatest remaining variance at a right angle to the first, and so on. Keeping only the first few components often preserves most of the information in a fraction of the dimensions.

Mechanically, PCA computes the covariance matrix of the standardized data and finds its eigenvectors, which become the component directions, and eigenvalues, which measure how much variance each direction carries. Projecting the data onto the top components yields the compressed representation. Each component's share of total variance tells you exactly how much information you keep: if three components carry 95 percent of the variance in a 50-feature dataset, the other 47 dimensions were mostly redundancy, since correlated features collapse into shared directions.

PCA serves three everyday purposes: visualization, projecting high-dimensional data to two or three plottable dimensions; preprocessing, shrinking feature counts to speed up and stabilize downstream models; and noise reduction, since low-variance directions often carry noise rather than signal. Its limits are linearity, as it only finds straight-line directions, and interpretability, since components are blends of original features. Reach for PCA when features are numerous and correlated, when you need a 2D view of high-dimensional data, or as a cheap preprocessing step, keeping in mind that maximum variance is not always maximum relevance for your prediction task.`,
    analogy: "PCA is choosing the best camera angle for photographing a complex sculpture: one viewpoint cannot capture everything, so you pick the angles that reveal the most shape, which is exactly what the data looks like along its directions of greatest variation.",
    visualExample: "Population geneticists run PCA on hundreds of thousands of genetic markers per person; the first two components alone, plotted on a scatter chart, famously reproduce a rough map of geographic ancestry within continents.",
  },

  nb: {
    explanation: `Naive Bayes classifies by applying Bayes' theorem with one bold simplification: it assumes every feature is independent of every other feature, given the class. To classify an email, it asks how likely each word is to appear in spam versus legitimate mail, multiplies those per-word probabilities together with the base rate of spam, and picks the class with the higher combined probability. The independence assumption is what makes the multiplication legitimate, and it is almost always false, which is why the method is called naive.

Training is little more than counting. For each class, count how often each feature value appears, convert the counts to probabilities, and store them. There is no iterative optimization, so training is nearly instantaneous even on huge datasets, and the model updates trivially as new data arrives. A small technical necessity is smoothing: a word never seen in spam during training would otherwise contribute a zero probability and veto the entire product, so every count is padded slightly.

The surprise is how well it works despite the false assumption. Classification only requires ranking the correct class above the others, not estimating probabilities precisely, and the independence errors often distort all classes similarly, leaving the ranking intact. Naive Bayes was the backbone of early spam filtering and remains strong for text tasks, where the bag-of-words representation suits it naturally. Reach for naive Bayes when you need a fast, simple baseline for text classification, when training data is limited, or when training and prediction speed matter more than squeezing out the last points of accuracy.`,
    analogy: "Naive Bayes judges like a doctor who checks each symptom against the disease base rates independently: fever alone suggests flu weakly, cough alone weakly too, but many weak independent clues multiplied together produce a confident diagnosis.",
    visualExample: "Classic spam filters are naive Bayes: words like 'winner', 'free', and 'urgent' each carry a learned spam probability, and multiplying the probabilities of all words in an incoming email yields a spam score, retrained continually from users hitting the report button.",
  },

  dl: {
    explanation: `Deep learning is machine learning using neural networks with many layers. Each layer transforms its input and passes the result to the next, and depth is the point: early layers learn simple patterns, later layers compose them into increasingly abstract ones. In an image network, the first layers detect edges, middle layers combine edges into textures and parts, and deep layers recognize whole objects. Crucially, none of these features are designed by humans; they emerge from training.

This is the decisive break from classical ML, which requires people to engineer input features by hand. Deep networks learn their own representations directly from raw data, pixels, audio waveforms, or text tokens. Training works by backpropagation: the network makes a prediction, a loss function scores the error, and calculus traces how much each of the millions or billions of weights contributed to that error, so each can be nudged in the direction that reduces it. Repeat across enormous datasets on GPUs, and the network gradually organizes itself into a hierarchy of useful features.

Deep learning is behind essentially every headline AI capability: language models, image generation, speech recognition, protein structure prediction, and game-playing agents. Its scaling behavior is its most consequential property, as performance keeps improving predictably with more data, more parameters, and more compute, which turned AI progress into an industrial effort. The costs are real: heavy data and compute requirements, and opaque internal reasoning. For tabular business data, classical methods often still win; deep learning's dominance is on raw perceptual and sequential data, where hand-crafting features was never feasible.`,
    analogy: "A deep network learns the way a film student becomes a critic: first noticing raw shapes and cuts, then shot composition, then scene structure, then meaning, with each level of understanding built out of the level below, from nothing but exposure to many examples.",
    visualExample: "When you say 'set a timer' to your phone, a deep network converts the raw audio waveform to text with no hand-written sound rules; its early layers respond to frequencies, middle layers to phonemes, and later layers to words, all learned from millions of hours of speech.",
  },
};
